  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040800/assets/d0e2bd46bf7db6e20f321383e8e0440a2a86f263ce5eb256d20db71f5c632e27.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=6{x}^{2}-10\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?",
  "worked_solution": "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=6{x}^{2}-10,\\,a=2,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}6{x}^{2}-10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{3}-10x]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 27-10\\cdot 3)-(2\\cdot 8-10\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $28\\,\\text{kg}.$",
